because every auto company,
every pharma company,

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:02.520
every retail company
needs to hire tech people.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:03.660
So to some degree,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.240
we should not think about
automation in one company

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.320
to mean that that person
is not required elsewhere.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.240
And so the question is what are,

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:16.800
what is the overall capacity
for the economy and society

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.440
to absorb and create new jobs?

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.960
And that I think is going to be the real.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:24.240
And then the other thing
that we should also have is

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.400
wage support for different jobs.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:30.420
I've always felt like why is
there such disparity today

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.440
in the job,

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:35.100
in the labor market between
let's say some care worker

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:38.700
and let's call it sort
of a software developer.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:42.000
And it's because we think of,

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:45.840
we as a society have decided
to give it different,

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:47.580
I'd say premium.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:51.360
Those premiums will adjust
as some of these technologies

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.880
really truly get diffused.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:55.860
- Well, the diffusion seems key

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.360
when I think about the theme this

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.040
here at Davos of fractionalization,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.440
and there's a lot of
talk in the hallways here

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:03.000
about the new globalization,

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:07.020
the implication of the
technology obviously go

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.780
beyond national borders quite
quickly and quite dramatically

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.360
when you talk about the
impact you see in India,

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:16.380
it's not just software jobs
moving from Silicon Valley

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:17.520
to Boise, Idaho,

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.920
but really potentially everywhere

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.180
around the world very quickly here.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:22.620
- Yeah, I mean look,

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:29.940
the idea that every country
has to have social cohesion

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:33.300
based on the jobs and
the economic opportunity,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.600
I think is much better
understood today than,

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.940
let's call it the first
phase of globalization.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.220
Whatever happens in the sort of

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:44.580
this phase of globalization is
not gonna repeat the mistakes

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:46.320
of the previous era

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.840
where when there is displacement
happening in a society,

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.760
I think everybody's gonna focus on

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:53.100
what do we do about those people
who are getting displaced,

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.600
getting trained, getting new jobs.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:58.200
So I think now we have to, in some sense,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:00.780
to your point, really
account for both forces.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.720
A globalization force, you know,

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:05.160
David Ricardo was not wrong.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.160
There is going to be
competitive advantage of nations

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.260
and things will still exist

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.840
where there is a reason why people

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:15.420
will wanna globalize,
but at the same time,

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:19.320
every politician gets elected
because of local politics

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.200
and they want to care a lot
more about jobs locally.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.840
And so therefore, in fact,
that's the right balance to have.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:26.820
- Last question on this,

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:28.440
we wanna talk about a few other things,

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:31.860
but it's a bit hypothetical, of course,

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:34.140
particularly given the scale
of change of the technology.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:37.680
But have you wrapped your head
around it as you look at it,

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:39.600
if you thought about a
company like Microsoft

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.580
five years from now, 10 years from now,

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:46.500
what this widespread adoption
of the technology of Chat GPT

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:49.500
means for how you're organized,
how you're structured,

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:50.460
how you do work,

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.480
what the real actual
implications are gonna be

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:55.200
on the ground.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.060
Have you been able to think
that through very much?

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:58.560
And when you look ahead, what do you see?

00:15:58.560 --> 00:15:59.100
- Absolutely.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:04.140
I mean this is the, one of the
hardest things that you learn

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:06.840
in a tech company.

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.800
You know, we are what, 48 years old today.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.820
And it's hard because you have to adjust,

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:16.980
the means of production essentially

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:21.120
in our tech business changed dramatically.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:25.140
And that it requires us
to unlearn and learn,

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.860
which is always very
unforgiving, quite frankly.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:32.100
And if you look at what
even these large models

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.080
or foundational models
are, it's very different.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:36.300
In fact, I would say one of the things

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.340
that is perhaps the toughest thing is

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:42.420
the previous era AI expert is the one

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.720
that is gonna have the
hardest time shifting

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:47.940
to this new paradigm because everything,

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:51.060
all the assumptions, oh
my God, here's my data,

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.500
I'm gonna train on that data.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:54.600
My data is the only thing that matters.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.400
All that's now questioned,

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.460
you now have a large
model which can be used

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.320
as a retrieval engine on your data

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:03.660
that's gonna perform better than sort of

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.360
what something that you
trained on your data.

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:07.740
I mean, just the intuition,

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:09.300
just so that people have what this is,

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.420
is suppose you train
a model on, let's say,

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:14.100
all the math formulas.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:16.620
Yeah, it'll get pretty efficient in math.

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:20.220
But let's say you train a
model on all the math formulas

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:23.460
and all the human text
there is on the web,

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:25.260
it gets better at math.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.000
And you say, why?

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.140
How is that possible?

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.300
Of course it's possible
because when we went to school,

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:32.880
it's not like we just learned
math, we learned math,

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:34.380
we learned history, we learned language.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.000
And so yeah, so something,

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:40.560
so that emergent capability
is a pretty different thing

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.620
than like why we were
even a couple of years ago

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:46.140
as state of the art in terms of AI.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:47.760
- Do we need to learn math anymore?

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.100
Why learn math?

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.580
- Lemme tell you,

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:52.140
I'm an electrical engineer

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.540
who never understood
the Maxwell's equations.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:58.380
So now I finally get, thanks to Chat GPT,

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:00.240
a better understanding of it.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:04.020
So I think one of the
things is we will all enjoy

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:07.680
a lot more math because we will
have that personalized tutor

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:12.060
who will, in fact, be able to
intervene at the crucial time

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:15.480
when you're making a conceptual
mistake and help you learn.

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:17.460
So just imagine that, just Matt,

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:21.300
what if there was a
fantastic tutor teacher

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.940
for every student learning
math going forward,

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:25.760
that is now possible.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:27.300
- Okay.

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:29.880
I'm gonna break my word 'cause I wanted

