one versus the other.

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:09.720
So I don't think there's
any going back to 2019,

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.220
but at the same time,

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:12.600
it's not like we are gonna be working

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:13.920
as if we are in 2020.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.500
And I think that that combination is

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:17.520
what we are all trying to crack.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:20.880
- I'm gonna go out for a couple questions

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:22.080
from the crowd in one second.

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:23.640
I'm gonna ask you one
thing before I do that,

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:24.720
I just wanted to get everybody ready

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.100
and then I know you're gonna have to run,

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:29.580
but I can't not ask you about Activision.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.520
The FTC is sued to stop the purchase.

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:35.940
You and Brad Smith have expressed
optimism about the deal.

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:38.580
There was a report from
a competitor of ours,

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:40.560
Reuters yesterday that the EU is looking

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:41.520
at challenging the deal.

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:44.580
Realistically, and I know you publicly

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:46.080
are expressing optimism about it.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:50.280
What's the timeframe for
a make or break point

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:52.740
on really pursuing this deal in your mind?

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:54.960
And are there conditions

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.180
that just would make it
impossible to pursue?

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.940
- Look, I mean the,

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:04.380
being whatever, a number
four player trying

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:10.200
to add some content and
create more opportunity

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:16.258
for more publishers, more
gamers to be able to enjoy the-

00:26:16.258 --> 00:26:18.540
- I don't think Leader Khan
is watching the livestream.

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:19.380
I have to warn you about.

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:21.660
- I mean, it's like if you
believe in competition,

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:23.760
you should believe in this deal.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:25.980
And I think the, you know,

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:29.280
the fact that people who
don't even make games

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.640
are the largest extractors
of rents in gaming,

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:34.380
shouldn't that tell the regulators

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:35.820
that where they should be looking.

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:42.780
It's unbelievable to me how,
you know, shortsighted some of,

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:47.460
I would say the way people
look at competition is,

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:48.180
and in fact,

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:52.560
I hope the competition
authorities get focused more

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.660
on competition and that'll be a good day.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:56.100
- Okay.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:57.360
Question right here.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:57.896
- [Keith] Hi Satya.

00:26:57.896 --> 00:26:58.320
Keith Ferrazzi.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:00.720
Oh hi Keith Ferrazzi.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.000
I run a research institute
on the future of hybrid work.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:06.780
It's very frustrating that
fewer than 15% of your peers

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:09.840
during this amazing remote
laboratory that they had

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:13.500
for two years actually looked
at reinventing how they work.

00:27:13.500 --> 00:27:15.840
And they still debate
where they're working,

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:18.660
but they haven't used your
extraordinary tech stack

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:20.640
to actually reinvent the work.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:22.980
What can you do to build a movement

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:25.500
that allows all the tools you have

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:29.040
to actually get used when
reality organizations

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:30.840
are merely scratching the surface?

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:32.700
- I mean, I think it's like,

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:34.140
it looks like what you are doing.

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.812
(Matt giggles)

00:27:35.812 --> 00:27:37.260
I mean, quite honestly, I think the,

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:40.320
I think it's a great
point, which is what's the,

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:41.640
if you take go back

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.640
to something like business
process re-engineering

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:45.720
in the early '90s,

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:48.060
there was very important

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:50.760
to have essentially a
management doctrine even

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:54.600
in order to help people
deploy the ERP systems

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:55.380
and what have you.

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:58.200
So the question is what
is the sophistication

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:02.040
of the sort of modern work
doctrine, so to speak,

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:06.960
that is much more widely
spread across all teams.

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.120
And so that's, I think what
everybody's understanding

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.420
and this is where the complexity
here is we have refined,

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:13.080
as somebody said,

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:16.020
the best collaboration
tool we have all developed

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:19.500
is the workplace for 200 plus years.

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:22.660
And so to suddenly say let's go add to it

00:28:22.660 --> 00:28:23.760
is sort of a tough challenge.

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.200
And so, but that's the challenge.

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.960
I think we have to learn
the management techniques,

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:31.140
like even how to hold
a meeting going forward

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:35.760
where there are two people
remote and nine people, say,

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:36.540
in the meeting.

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:39.480
The expectations of those two people

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:43.260
are very different today
than they were two years ago.

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.420
They're not going to deal
with anything second class.

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:48.300
And so if you as a leader
don't have the skills

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:50.820
on how to incorporate them appropriately,

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:51.900
you're gonna have a bad meeting.

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:55.260
And so I think that that
understanding, like, you know,

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:58.140
it's not like the thing that
you learn when you join,

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:00.840
but I think people who are
growing up as leaders today,

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.460
and you know workers today

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.020
are going to learn that inherently.

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:05.700
And so it's a generational change.

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:08.520
- Gentlemen, right there, the white shirt.

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:12.507
Okay, this way next.

00:29:12.507 --> 00:29:14.700
- [Philip]&nbsp;&nbsp;

